# Profile overrides for elitebook-thermal-profile.
#
# Copy to /etc/elitebook-thermal-profile/profiles.conf and edit. Package
# upgrades never overwrite that file, so changes made here survive updates,
# unlike editing the installed script.
#
# Only write down what you want to change. Anything absent keeps the built-in
# value, so an empty file behaves exactly like no file at all.
#
# Keys are <PROFILE>_<VALUE>:
#
#   profiles  AC, PERFORMANCE, BATTERY, BATTERY_SAVER, GAMING, COOL
#   values    STAPM_MW, FAST_MW, SLOW_MW, APU_MW   power, in milliwatts
#             TCTL_C                               thermal target, in Celsius
#             EPP                                  performance,
#                                                  balance_performance,
#                                                  balance_power, power
#
# Accepted ranges: power 4000-45000 mW, TCTL_C 60-100. A value outside its
# range, or one that is not a number, is refused with a message on stderr and
# the built-in default is used instead: a typo here cannot leave the machine
# untuned, and cannot be used to set a limit the chassis has no chance of
# dissipating.
#
# Raising these numbers above the defaults moves the machine back towards the
# stock behaviour this project exists to avoid. Lowering them is the safe
# direction.
#
# After editing:
#   sudo elitebook-thermal-profile auto      # apply
#   elitebook-thermal-profile status         # confirm what is in force
#
# A smaller chassis, or a 6-core part, may not hold the sustained envelope
# that was measured on an 845 G8 with a Ryzen 7 PRO 5850U:
#AC_SLOW_MW=15000
#AC_APU_MW=15000

# A quieter thermal target for calls or hot rooms:
#COOL_TCTL_C=80

# More conservative battery behaviour:
#BATTERY_SLOW_MW=12000
#BATTERY_EPP=power
